      "text": "To convert from spherical coordinates \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)\\) to rectangular coordinates \\((x, y, z)\\), we use the following equations:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nx &= \\rho \\sin \\phi \\cos \\theta \\\\\ny &= \\rho \\sin \\phi \\sin \\theta \\\\\nz &= \\rho \\cos \\phi\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nIn this problem, the spherical coordinates are \\((\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) = \\left( 3, \\frac{5 \\pi}{12}, 0 \\right)\\). Substituting these values into the equations, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\nx &= 3 \\sin 0 \\cos \\frac{5\\pi}{12} \\\\\ny &= 3 \\sin 0 \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} \\\\\nz &= 3 \\cos 0\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nFirst, we calculate \\(x\\): since \\(\\rho = 3\\), \\(\\tan \\theta = \\frac{y}{x}\\), and \\(\\phi = 0\\), we have \\(\\rho \\sin \\phi = 3 \\sin 0 = 0\\) and \\(\\cos \\phi = \\cos 0 = 1\\). This means \\(\\phi = 0\\) which implies \\(\\sin \\phi = 0\\) and \\(\\cos \\phi = 1\\), simplifying the calculation to \\(x = 3 \\cdot 0 \\cdot \\cos \\frac{5\\pi}{12} = 0\\). \n\nNext, we calculate \\(y\\): since \\(\\phi = 0\\) and \\(\\rho = 3\\), we have \\(\\rho \\sin \\phi = 3 \\sin 0 = 0\\) and \\(\\cos \\phi = 1\\). This means \\(\\phi = 0\\) which implies \\(\\sin \\phi = 0\\) and \\(\\cos \\phi = 1\\), simplifying the calculation to \\(y = 3 \\cdot 0 \\cdot \\sin \\frac{5\\pi}{12} = 0\\).\n\nLastly, we calculate \\(z\\): since \\(\\phi = 0\\) and \\(\\rho = 3\\), we have \\(\\rho \\sin \\phi = 3 \\sin 0 = 0\\) and \\(\\cos \\phi = 1\\). This means \\(z = 3 \\cdot 1 = 3\\).\n\nPutting it all together, the rectangular coordinates are \\((x, y, z) = (0, 0, 3)\\).\n\nTherefore, the answer is \\(\\boxed{(0, 0, 3)}\\).",
